## Alarming Trends in Food Recalls

The food supply chain, ever intricate, has shown its cracks again as we tally up the 337 recalls so far this year, 91 of which are Class I, signaling the most serious health risks. **Among the repeat offenders, Moonlight Packing Corporation stands out, with a frustratingly high count of 11 recalls, all Class I.**

## This Week's Highlights

Fresh off the recall list are some eyebrow-raising items:

- **Negita Cracker - Shrimp 2.8oz**: That golden hue? Thanks to undeclared FD&C Yellow 6.
- **52USA brand Popping Boba (multiple flavors)**: Passion fruit, mango, strawberry--each with a side of unapproved colors like tartrazine (FD&C Yellow 5).
- **Specially SELECTED Vanilla Creme Brulee**: May contain pieces of glass.
- **Einstein Bros Bagels Honey Almond Double-Whipped Shmear**: Labeling mismatch means undeclared almond allergen.
- **Hemu Trading Inc's Enoki Mushrooms**: A concerning Class I recall due to Listeria monocytogenes.

## Deeper Dive: Undeclared and Unapproved

The rise in recalls involving undeclared allergens and unapproved additives is concerning. Allergen recalls alone constitute 188 of this year's incidents. Unapproved ingredients, like the synthetic colors flagged in Popping Boba, reflect systemic issues in ingredient sourcing and compliance oversight--particularly in imports.

## Repeat Offenders

Moonlight Packing Corporation isn't the only brand that's persistently appearing on the recall list. The likes of Albertsons Companies LLC and Diva Fam Inc share the ignominious spotlight.

Brands like **CHETAK NEW YORK LLC** and **Made Fresh Salads Inc** also contribute heavily to the total recall count. Are these lapses indicative of systemic deficiencies?

## The Verdict

This week's recalls underscore the need for vigilance. Whether it's for a midday snack or adding to your dinner menu, double-check your products against recall lists. Avoid repeat offenders and advocate for stricter quality control.
